-- AWS SCS Domain 3 (Infrastructure Security) expansion: adds flashcards sc-f-400..420/sc-a-140..149/sc-y-070..075 and questions sc-q-400..430 for objectives 3.1 (edge: WAF Web ACLs/managed rule groups/rate-based rules/CAPTCHA, Shield Advanced protected resources/Shield Response Team, Firewall Manager prerequisites), 3.2 (network: NACL rule evaluation order/SG defaults and self-reference, Gateway Load Balancer, Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall, PrivateLink vs VPC peering, NAT Gateway vs NAT instance), and 3.3 (compute: SSM prerequisites/session logging/Run Command/Patch Manager/State Manager/session restriction/port forwarding, IMDSv2/SSRF/hop-limit).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('sc-f-400','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a Web ACL in AWS WAF?$$,$$The Web Access Control List - a container of rules and rule groups attached to a resource (CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway, AppSync, or Cognito user pool) that evaluates requests in priority order and applies a default action (allow or block) to anything not matched by an earlier rule.$$),
('sc-f-401','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What are AWS Managed Rules rule groups in WAF?$$,$$Pre-built, continuously updated rule groups maintained by AWS or AWS Marketplace sellers (e.g., Core rule set, SQL database, Known Bad Inputs, Bot Control, Account Takeover Prevention) that you add to a Web ACL instead of authoring and maintaining custom rules yourself.$$),
('sc-f-402','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What does an AWS WAF rate-based rule do?$$,$$Tracks the request rate from an aggregation key (typically source IP) over a rolling 5-minute window and triggers an action - block, count, or CAPTCHA - once a configured threshold is exceeded, mitigating Layer 7 floods and brute-force attempts.$$),
('sc-f-403','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What do the WAF CAPTCHA and Challenge rule actions provide?$$,$$An interactive (CAPTCHA) or silent, non-interactive (Challenge) client-side verification step inserted as a rule action, letting legitimate clients continue after proving they aren't automated - a milder response than an outright block.$$),
('sc-f-404','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$Which resource types can AWS Shield Advanced directly protect?$$,$$CloudFront distributions, Global Accelerator accelerators, Route 53 hosted zones, Elastic Load Balancers (ALB/NLB/CLB), and EC2 Elastic IP addresses - the internet-facing edge and network entry points of an application.$$),
('sc-f-405','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the AWS Shield Response Team (SRT)?$$,$$A team available 24/7 to Shield Advanced subscribers with an SRT support plan, who can be engaged during an active DDoS attack to help write and tune mitigating WAF rules in real time.$$),
('sc-f-406','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What must be in place before AWS Firewall Manager can automatically enforce policies across every account and future resource in an Organization?$$,$$AWS Organizations must be set up with a delegated Firewall Manager administrator account, and AWS Config must be enabled - Firewall Manager relies on it to discover resources and track compliance. Once configured, policies such as WAF, Shield Advanced, security group, and Network Firewall auto-apply to new accounts and resources with no manual step.$$),
('sc-f-407','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$How does a Network ACL evaluate its rules, and why must both inbound and outbound rules be defined?$$,$$Rules are evaluated in ascending rule-number order, and the first matching rule wins - the rest are never evaluated for that packet. Because NACLs are stateless (unlike security groups), return traffic isn't automatically permitted, so both the inbound request and the corresponding outbound response (including ephemeral ports) must be explicitly allowed.$$),
('sc-f-408','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a security group's default behavior for inbound and outbound traffic?$$,$$New security groups implicitly deny all inbound traffic until allow rules are added, but allow all outbound traffic by default. Security groups support only Allow rules (no explicit Deny) and are stateful, so return traffic for an allowed connection is automatically permitted in the reverse direction.$$),
('sc-f-409','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$What is an AWS Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) used for?$$,$$Transparently distributes traffic across a scalable fleet of third-party virtual security appliances (firewalls, IDS/IPS, deep packet inspection tools) inline in the traffic path, using GENEVE encapsulation and flow stickiness so a given connection consistently reaches the same appliance instance.$$),
('sc-f-410','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$What does Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall do?$$,$$Filters outbound DNS queries from resources inside a VPC against domain allow/deny lists - including AWS-managed threat-intelligence lists - blocking resolution of known-malicious domains and stopping DNS-based command-and-control or exfiltration channels before a connection is even attempted.$$),
('sc-f-411','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$How does AWS PrivateLink differ from VPC peering for reaching a service in another account's VPC?$$,$$PrivateLink exposes one specific service through an interface endpoint (an ENI) in the consumer's VPC, giving one-directional access with no route table changes and no concern about overlapping CIDR ranges. VPC peering instead fully connects the two VPCs' networks, requires non-overlapping CIDRs and route table entries, and exposes far more than a single service.$$),
('sc-f-412','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$What must be in place on an EC2 instance for SSM Session Manager to manage it?$$,$$The SSM Agent running on the instance, an attached IAM instance profile granting Systems Manager permissions (typically the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy), and outbound network reachability to the Systems Manager service - either via the internet/NAT or via VPC interface endpoints (ssm, ssmmessages, ec2messages) for fully private subnets.$$),
('sc-f-413','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$Where is Session Manager session activity logged?$$,$$Session content (commands and output) can be streamed to a CloudWatch Logs log group and/or an S3 bucket, optionally encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key, while session start/stop events and the initiating IAM principal are separately recorded in CloudTrail.$$),
('sc-f-414','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$What is AWS Systems Manager Run Command?$$,$$A capability that executes predefined or custom commands and scripts on managed instances at scale through an IAM-controlled API call, without needing SSH/RDP access or an interactive session on each host.$$),
('sc-f-415','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$What is AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager?$$,$$Automates scanning managed instances against a patch baseline and installing missing OS/application patches on a defined schedule, then reports compliance status so unpatched hosts - a common attack vector - can be found and remediated fleet-wide.$$),
('sc-f-416','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$What is AWS Systems Manager State Manager?$$,$$Continuously enforces a defined configuration state (such as a required security agent running or a specific setting present) on managed instances through associations, automatically remediating any detected drift back to the desired state.$$),
('sc-f-417','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$How can you restrict which principals may start a Session Manager session, and on which instances or session types?$$,$$IAM policies can condition ssm:StartSession on resource tags (limiting which instances a principal may connect to) and on the specific SSM document used to start the session - allowing standard shell access while denying riskier session types such as port forwarding.$$),
('sc-f-418','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$What is Session Manager port forwarding?$$,$$Tunnels a local port to a specific port on the remote managed instance (such as RDP or a database port) through the existing Systems Manager channel, providing remote access to that service without opening any inbound security group rule.$$),
('sc-f-419','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$What is IMDSv2, and why does it matter for compute security?$$,$$A session-oriented mode of the EC2 Instance Metadata Service that requires a PUT request to obtain a token before any GET request will return data, closing the SSRF-to-credential-theft path that IMDSv1 left open, where a simple GET alone could retrieve the attached role's temporary credentials.$$),
('sc-f-420','awsscs','core',3,'3.3',$$How does the http-put-response-hop-limit setting strengthen IMDSv2's protection?$$,$$It caps how many network hops the token-request PUT can traverse before being dropped; setting it to 1 prevents a process running one hop away - such as inside a container on the same instance - from forwarding the metadata token request to obtain credentials meant only for the host itself.$$),
('sc-a-140','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.1',$$SRT$$,$$Shield Response Team - the 24/7 team available to AWS Shield Advanced subscribers for hands-on help mitigating an active DDoS attack.$$),
('sc-a-141','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.1',$$DDoS$$,$$Distributed Denial of Service - an attack that overwhelms a resource's capacity from many distributed sources at once; mitigated in AWS with Shield, WAF, and architectural absorption via CloudFront/Global Accelerator.$$),
('sc-a-142','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.1',$$CAPTCHA$$,$$Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart - an interactive verification challenge; AWS WAF can apply a CAPTCHA action to requests matching a rule instead of blocking them outright.$$),
('sc-a-143','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.2',$$GWLB$$,$$Gateway Load Balancer - a Layer 3/4 load balancer that transparently scales and load-balances traffic across third-party inline virtual appliances such as firewalls and intrusion prevention systems.$$),
('sc-a-144','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.2',$$NAT$$,$$Network Address Translation - translates private instance addresses to a shared address for outbound-only internet access, implemented in AWS via a NAT Gateway or self-managed NAT instance.$$),
('sc-a-145','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.2',$$CIDR$$,$$Classless Inter-Domain Routing - the slash-notation IP address range format (e.g., 10.0.0.0/16) used to define the address space of a VPC, subnet, or security rule.$$),
('sc-a-146','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.3',$$IMDS$$,$$Instance Metadata Service - the in-instance HTTP endpoint (169.254.169.254) exposing an EC2 instance's identity, configuration, and the attached role's temporary credentials; IMDSv2 requires a token-based session to access it.$$),
('sc-a-147','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.3',$$SSRF$$,$$Server-Side Request Forgery - an attack that tricks a server into making requests chosen by the attacker; on EC2, historically used against IMDSv1 to steal instance role credentials with a single crafted request.$$),
('sc-a-148','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.3',$$RDP$$,$$Remote Desktop Protocol - Windows remote graphical access; can be reached through an SSM Session Manager port-forwarding tunnel instead of opening an inbound security group rule.$$),
('sc-a-149','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.3',$$AMI$$,$$Amazon Machine Image - the template (OS, packages, configuration) used to launch EC2 instances; building a hardened, minimal "golden AMI" is a core host-hardening practice.$$),
-- feynman
('sc-y-070','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain how a Web ACL, rule groups, and managed rules fit together in AWS WAF.$$,$$Model answer: The Web ACL is the top-level container attached to a protected resource like a CloudFront distribution or ALB - it holds an ordered list of rules and rule groups, evaluated by priority, plus a default action for anything nothing else matches. Individual rules can be custom (a rate-based rule, an IP set match, a geo-match) or bundled into rule groups, and AWS Managed Rules are pre-built rule groups - covering things like SQL injection patterns or known bad bots - that AWS maintains and updates for you, so you get continuously current protection by simply adding the group rather than writing and maintaining every signature yourself. Evaluation stops at the first rule with a terminating action (block or allow), so priority order matters.$$),
('sc-y-071','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain why Shield Advanced's cost protection and Shield Response Team engagement matter together during a large-scale DDoS attack on a public web app.$$,$$Model answer: A big DDoS attack threatens a company two ways at once - the app might go down, and even if auto-scaling keeps it up, the bill for all that extra CloudFront/ELB/EC2 capacity spun up specifically because of the attack can spike hugely. Cost protection addresses the second threat directly: it credits back scaling charges attributable to the attack, so surviving the attack doesn't also mean eating an unexpected bill. The Shield Response Team addresses the first threat: during an active attack, their engineers help write and tune WAF rules in real time, which is exactly the expertise a company's own team may not have on hand at 2 a.m. Together, one protects the wallet and the other protects the uptime - financial risk and operational risk from the same event, covered by two different pieces of Shield Advanced.$$),
('sc-y-072','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain why a security group alone isn't enough network protection for a multi-tier VPC, and what NACLs and Network Firewall each add on top.$$,$$Model answer: A security group is stateful and instance-level, but it only supports allow rules and doesn't inspect packet contents - if a rule is scoped too broadly (say, an entire tier's security group as a source), anything within that tier can reach anything else in it, and nothing about the SG stops a malicious payload riding on an otherwise-permitted port. A NACL adds a second, independent boundary at the subnet level: because it's stateless and separately configured, a mistake or compromise at the security-group layer doesn't automatically bypass it, and it's convenient for broadly blocking known-bad ranges across an entire subnet regardless of what individual instance security groups say. AWS Network Firewall adds a third layer that neither of the others can provide: actual deep packet inspection with IPS-style signatures and domain filtering at the VPC boundary, catching payload-based threats and malicious destinations that pure IP/port filtering would let straight through. Each layer catches something the one before it structurally cannot.$$),
('sc-y-073','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain step by step how SSM Session Manager gets a shell to a private EC2 instance with zero inbound security group rules.$$,$$Model answer: It starts on the instance's side - the SSM Agent, running under the instance's IAM role, opens an outbound HTTPS connection to the Systems Manager service (either over the internet/NAT or via VPC interface endpoints if the subnet is fully private) and registers itself as a managed instance. That connection is initiated by the instance, so nothing needs to be opened for inbound traffic at all. When an authorized user calls ssm:StartSession, Systems Manager brokers a session over that same outbound channel back to the user's console or CLI - the instance never accepts a new inbound connection, it just carries traffic over the connection it already opened. Every byte typed and returned in that channel can be streamed to CloudWatch Logs or S3 for a full audit trail, and the only gatekeeper the whole way through is IAM policy - no SSH keys, no open port 22, and no bastion host in the path at all.$$),
('sc-y-074','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain why IMDSv2 closes an SSRF attack path that IMDSv1 left open.$$,$$Model answer: IMDSv1 will hand back an instance's metadata - including its attached role's temporary credentials - to any plain GET request sent to the metadata address, no session or prior step required. If an application on the instance has a server-side request forgery bug (it fetches a URL the attacker gets to influence), the attacker just points that fetch at the metadata address and receives valid AWS credentials back in the response body. IMDSv2 changes the protocol: you must first send a PUT request to obtain a short-lived session token, and only a GET carrying that token in a header will succeed. Most SSRF bugs let an attacker control the URL of an outgoing GET request, not fabricate an entire PUT-then-token-header GET sequence with a custom header - so requiring that token exchange breaks the typical SSRF-to-credential-theft chain even though the underlying SSRF bug in the application still technically exists.$$),
('sc-y-075','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain how Patch Manager, State Manager, and Session Manager together form a host-hardening strategy that doesn't rely on SSH.$$,$$Model answer: think of it as prevention, self-healing, and a safety net. Patch Manager handles prevention at the OS/software level - it keeps known CVEs closed across the fleet on a schedule, before an attacker can exploit something already fixed upstream. State Manager handles self-healing - it continuously checks that the required configuration (security agents running, required settings present) actually holds, and corrects drift automatically instead of waiting for a human to notice. Session Manager is the safety net for the rare cases where a human genuinely needs to get onto a box - and even then, it's an IAM-governed, fully logged channel with no inbound port and no SSH key ever handed out. Put together, the fleet mostly hardens and heals itself automatically, and the few times a person does need in, that access is exactly as controlled and auditable as everything else.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- Objective 3.1: edge security (WAF, Shield, Firewall Manager)
('sc-q-400','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$Which AWS WAF construct is attached directly to a resource such as a CloudFront distribution or Application Load Balancer, and holds the ordered list of rules and rule groups that decide whether to allow or block each request?$q$,$q$["A Web ACL","A security group","A Network ACL","An AWS Firewall Manager policy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A Web ACL (Web Access Control List) is the WAF resource attached to CloudFront/ALB/API Gateway/AppSync that holds rules and rule groups evaluated in priority order, with a default action. Security groups and NACLs are network-layer controls with no Layer 7 rule evaluation, and a Firewall Manager policy is what pushes and enforces Web ACLs across accounts - it isn't itself the per-resource rule container.$q$),
('sc-q-401','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$A team wants proven, continuously updated protection against common SQL injection and cross-site scripting patterns in a Web ACL, without writing and maintaining custom rules themselves. What should they add?$q$,$q$["An AWS Managed Rules rule group (e.g., the SQL database or Core rule set)","A NACL deny rule","A custom rate-based rule only","A Firewall Manager security group policy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AWS Managed Rules are pre-built, AWS- or Marketplace-maintained rule groups covering common exploit patterns; adding one to a Web ACL gets continuously updated protection with no custom rule authoring. A NACL rule operates below Layer 7 and can't inspect HTTP content, a rate-based rule only throttles by request volume, and a security group policy controls network reachability, not request content.$q$),
('sc-q-402','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$An application faces a credential-stuffing attack where a large botnet submits login attempts from thousands of constantly rotating source IP addresses, each at a rate too low individually to trip a volume threshold. Which AWS WAF managed rule group is purpose-built for this scenario?$q$,$q$["AWS WAF Bot Control","AWS WAF Account Takeover Prevention (ATP)","The Amazon IP reputation list managed rule group","The Core rule set (CRS)"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Account Takeover Prevention inspects login endpoint traffic and credential patterns to detect compromised-credential and credential-stuffing attempts regardless of how many different source IPs are used. Bot Control targets bad-bot traffic broadly rather than login-specific abuse, the IP reputation list blocks known-bad IPs (defeated here by IP rotation), and the Core rule set targets generic OWASP exploit patterns, not credential stuffing.$q$),
('sc-q-403','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$An AWS WAF rate-based rule counts requests from each aggregation key (such as source IP) over what kind of evaluation window before it can trigger its configured action?$q$,$q$["A fixed calendar 5-minute window that resets on the clock","A continuously rolling (sliding) 5-minute evaluation window","A rolling 24-hour window","A fixed 1-minute window"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rate-based rules evaluate a rolling, continuously updated 5-minute window of request counts per aggregation key, not a fixed clock-aligned window or a 24-hour or 1-minute period - this lets the rule react quickly to a sudden burst without waiting for a calendar boundary to reset.$q$),
('sc-q-404','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$Which AWS WAF rule action presents an interactive or silent verification step to distinguish a human or legitimate client from a bot, as a milder response than an outright block?$q$,$q$["CAPTCHA (or Challenge) action","A geo-match rule","An IP set match rule","A rate-based rule"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$WAF's CAPTCHA and Challenge actions insert a verification step for requests that match a rule, letting legitimate clients continue after proving they're not automated, without fully blocking the request outright. Geo-match filters by country, IP set rules match specific addresses, and rate-based rules act purely on request volume - none of them perform an interactive human/bot check.$q$),
('sc-q-405','awsscs',3,'3.1','multi',3,$q$Which of the following resource types can be directly protected by AWS Shield Advanced? (Select TWO)$q$,$q$["Amazon CloudFront distribution","AWS Lambda function (invoked directly, with no other front end)","Amazon EC2 Elastic IP address","Amazon S3 bucket (accessed directly via its REST endpoint)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Shield Advanced protects internet-facing edge and network resource types - CloudFront distributions, Global Accelerator accelerators, Route 53 hosted zones, Elastic Load Balancers, and EC2 Elastic IP addresses. A Lambda function invoked directly and an S3 bucket accessed directly are not Shield Advanced protected resource types; they gain DDoS protection only indirectly if fronted by a protected resource such as CloudFront.$q$),
('sc-q-406','awsscs',3,'3.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the layers of a typical defense-in-depth design for a public web application, from the outermost edge layer that traffic hits first to the innermost layer that finally runs business logic.$q$,$q$["AWS Shield absorbing and mitigating network/transport-layer DDoS traffic at the edge","Amazon CloudFront caching and distributing content from edge locations","AWS WAF evaluating each request's Web ACL rules at Layer 7","Application code executing on the origin server"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Shield's DDoS mitigation is automatically integrated at the network edge (with CloudFront, Route 53, and Global Accelerator) and absorbs volumetric attacks first; CloudFront then serves and caches legitimate content from edge locations, reducing what reaches further inward; WAF evaluates the surviving requests' Layer 7 content against the Web ACL's rules; and only requests that pass all of that reach the application code on the origin. Each layer filters the traffic the next layer has to handle.$q$),
('sc-q-407','awsscs',3,'3.1','matching',2,$q$Match each edge security service to the primary protection it provides.$q$,$q${"left":["AWS Shield Standard","AWS Shield Advanced","AWS WAF","AWS Firewall Manager"],"right":["Automatic, no-cost Layer 3/4 DDoS protection included for every AWS customer","Enhanced DDoS protection adding cost protection and Shield Response Team engagement","Layer 7 rule-based filtering of HTTP(S) requests via a Web ACL","Centralized deployment and enforcement of WAF, Shield Advanced, and security group policy across an Organization"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Shield Standard is automatic, free Layer 3/4 protection for all customers; Shield Advanced adds paid enhancements like DDoS cost protection and SRT access; WAF filters Layer 7 HTTP(S) content through Web ACLs; and Firewall Manager centrally rolls out and enforces all of these policies consistently across every account in an Organization.$q$),
('sc-q-408','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A company configures an AWS Firewall Manager WAF policy that must automatically attach a baseline Web ACL to every CloudFront distribution in every member account of its Organization, including accounts and distributions created in the future, with no manual step required per account. What must already be true for this to work?$q$,$q$["AWS Organizations is in use with a delegated Firewall Manager administrator account, and AWS Config is enabled to track resource compliance","Each account owner must manually attach the Web ACL themselves","Firewall Manager only supports enforcement within a single AWS account","Firewall Manager WAF policies cannot target CloudFront distributions"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Firewall Manager requires AWS Organizations with a delegated Firewall Manager administrator account, and relies on AWS Config to discover and track resource compliance so policies can auto-apply to new, in-scope resources without manual per-account work. Manual per-account attachment defeats the purpose of central enforcement, and Firewall Manager explicitly supports multi-account, organization-wide scope and does support targeting CloudFront distributions.$q$),
('sc-q-409','awsscs',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A security team wants to retain full request-level AWS WAF logs, including which rule matched and what action was taken, for later querying in a SIEM or with Amazon Athena. Which logging approach provides this?$q$,$q$["Configuring WAF logging to stream to a destination such as Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose (to S3) or CloudWatch Logs","Relying on VPC Flow Logs","Relying on CloudTrail management events","There is no way to log individual WAF request evaluations"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AWS WAF supports enabling logging that streams per-request log records - including matched rule and action - to a configured destination such as Kinesis Data Firehose (commonly landing in S3) or CloudWatch Logs, which can then be queried with Athena or ingested into a SIEM. VPC Flow Logs capture only network metadata, and CloudTrail management events record control-plane API calls, not individual WAF request evaluations.$q$),
-- Objective 3.2: network security (SG, NACL, Network Firewall, VPC endpoints, PrivateLink, NAT)
('sc-q-410','awsscs',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A Network ACL has inbound rule 100 set to ALLOW traffic from 0.0.0.0/0, and inbound rule 200 set to DENY traffic from 203.0.113.0/24. A packet arrives from 203.0.113.5. What happens?$q$,$q$["It is denied, because rule 200 is the more specific match","It is allowed, because NACL rules are evaluated in ascending numeric order and rule 100 matches first, so rule 200 is never reached","It is denied, because NACLs always prioritize DENY rules over ALLOW rules","The outcome depends entirely on the associated security group"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$NACLs evaluate rules strictly in ascending rule-number order and stop at the first match - rule 100's broad ALLOW is reached first and applies, so the more specific rule 200 never gets evaluated for this packet. NACLs don't prioritize by specificity or by rule type (ALLOW vs DENY); only the numeric order matters, which is why rule numbering strategy matters when designing NACLs.$q$),
('sc-q-411','awsscs',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$By default, can two EC2 instances that are members of the same security group communicate with each other over the network?$q$,$q$["Only if the security group has a rule explicitly allowing inbound traffic from that same security group as the source","Yes, security groups always allow all traffic between members of the same group automatically","No, traffic between members of the same security group is always blocked","Only if they are connected through a NAT Gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Security groups don't automatically permit traffic between their own members; the default-deny-inbound behavior still applies unless you add a rule whose source is the security group itself (a self-referencing rule), which is a common pattern for letting instances in an Auto Scaling group or cluster reach each other. It isn't automatic, isn't permanently blocked, and has nothing to do with NAT Gateways.$q$),
('sc-q-412','awsscs',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which AWS service transparently inserts a scalable fleet of third-party virtual security appliances (such as intrusion detection/prevention systems) inline in front of VPC workloads, using GENEVE encapsulation so the appliances see the original traffic without the workloads needing to know the appliances' addresses?$q$,$q$["Gateway Load Balancer","Application Load Balancer","NAT Gateway","AWS WAF"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Gateway Load Balancer operates at Layer 3/4 specifically to distribute traffic across a fleet of third-party virtual appliances using GENEVE encapsulation and flow stickiness, transparently inserting inspection into the traffic path. Application Load Balancer routes Layer 7 HTTP(S) traffic to application targets, a NAT Gateway only provides outbound internet access, and WAF filters HTTP requests rather than load-balancing appliance traffic.$q$),
('sc-q-413','awsscs',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$A security team wants to stop EC2 instances in a VPC from resolving DNS lookups for known malware command-and-control domains, blocking the callback before any network connection is even attempted. What should they configure?$q$,$q$["Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall with a threat-intelligence domain deny list applied to the VPC","A Network ACL deny rule","An AWS WAF geo-match rule","VPC Flow Logs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall filters outbound DNS queries from VPC resources against domain allow/deny lists - including AWS-managed threat-intelligence lists - blocking resolution of malicious domains before any connection is attempted. A NACL filters IP/port traffic, not DNS names; WAF filters HTTP(S) requests at Layer 7; and Flow Logs only record traffic metadata after the fact, they don't block anything.$q$),
('sc-q-414','awsscs',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A company needs private connectivity from its VPC to a specific service running in a partner's AWS account, without establishing full bidirectional network routing between the two VPCs and without needing to coordinate non-overlapping CIDR ranges. What should it use?$q$,$q$["VPC peering","AWS PrivateLink via an interface VPC endpoint","A Transit Gateway with routes propagated between both VPCs","A public NAT Gateway in each VPC"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$PrivateLink exposes a single service through an interface endpoint (an ENI) in the consumer's VPC, giving one-directional access to just that service with no CIDR overlap concerns and no route table changes. VPC peering and Transit Gateway both build full network-level connectivity between the VPCs (requiring non-overlapping CIDRs and exposing more than one service), and a NAT Gateway only enables outbound internet access, not private cross-account service connectivity.$q$),
('sc-q-415','awsscs',3,'3.2','multi',3,$q$Which of the following statements about interface VPC endpoints (AWS PrivateLink) are correct? (Select TWO)$q$,$q$["They are backed by an Elastic Network Interface (ENI) in your subnet, so a security group can be used to control access to them","They require adding route table entries that point at the endpoint","They can optionally be given a private DNS name that resolves to the endpoint's private IP addresses","They are limited to only Amazon S3 and DynamoDB as target services"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Interface endpoints are ENI-based, which is exactly why a security group can be attached to control access to them, and they support an optional private DNS feature so existing application code resolving the service's standard DNS name transparently reaches the endpoint. They do not require route table entries - that's how Gateway endpoints work instead - and the S3/DynamoDB-only limitation also describes Gateway endpoints, not interface endpoints, which support most other AWS and partner/customer services.$q$),
('sc-q-416','awsscs',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the key architectural difference between a self-managed NAT instance and a managed NAT Gateway?$q$,$q$["A NAT Gateway is a highly available, AWS-managed service with no security group of its own to configure, while a NAT instance is an EC2 instance you must patch, scale, and whose security group you manage yourself","A NAT instance always provides higher bandwidth than a NAT Gateway","A NAT Gateway requires you to install and configure NAT software on it","There is no meaningful difference between the two"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NAT Gateway is a fully managed, highly available AWS service (no security group attached to it directly, traffic is controlled via the subnet's NACLs and route tables) that scales automatically, while a NAT instance is an ordinary EC2 instance running NAT software that you must patch, scale, and secure with a security group yourself. Bandwidth generally favors the managed NAT Gateway, which also requires no software installation - the reverse of a NAT instance.$q$),
('sc-q-417','awsscs',3,'3.2','matching',2,$q$Match each network security control to its correct scope and behavior.$q$,$q${"left":["Security group","Network ACL","AWS Network Firewall","Gateway Load Balancer"],"right":["Stateful, instance-level, supports allow rules only","Stateless, subnet-level, evaluated in ascending rule-number order","Stateful deep packet inspection and domain filtering enforced at VPC scale","Transparently inserts third-party inline appliances into the traffic path via GENEVE encapsulation"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Security groups are stateful, instance-level, and allow-only; NACLs are stateless, subnet-level, and evaluated by ascending rule number; AWS Network Firewall provides stateful IPS-style inspection and domain filtering at the VPC boundary; and Gateway Load Balancer transparently threads traffic through third-party inline security appliances using GENEVE - four distinct layers of network control.$q$),
-- Objective 3.3: compute security (SSM Session Manager and related host-hardening)
('sc-q-418','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$Which AWS managed IAM policy is commonly attached to an EC2 instance's role to grant it the permissions needed to be managed through SSM Session Manager?$q$,$q$["AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore","AdministratorAccess","AmazonEC2FullAccess","ReadOnlyAccess"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore grants the minimum permissions the SSM Agent needs to register the instance and support Session Manager, Run Command, and related features. AdministratorAccess and AmazonEC2FullAccess grant far more than needed (violating least privilege), and ReadOnlyAccess doesn't permit the agent to communicate with Systems Manager.$q$),
('sc-q-419','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$EC2 instances in a fully private subnet with no NAT Gateway and no internet gateway must remain reachable through SSM Session Manager. What must be added to the VPC?$q$,$q$["Public IP addresses assigned to each instance","VPC interface endpoints for the ssm, ssmmessages, and ec2messages services","A bastion host in the private subnet","An internet gateway attached to the VPC"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Interface VPC endpoints for ssm, ssmmessages, and ec2messages let the SSM Agent reach the Systems Manager service entirely over the private AWS network, with no internet path required. Public IPs and an internet gateway would expose the instances directly to the internet (the opposite of the goal), and a bastion host is exactly the pattern Session Manager is meant to replace.$q$),
('sc-q-420','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$Which AWS Systems Manager capability lets an administrator execute a script across hundreds of managed instances simultaneously via an API call, without opening an interactive shell to any of them?$q$,$q$["Run Command","Session Manager's interactive shell","A patch baseline","Parameter Store"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Run Command executes predefined or custom commands/scripts across many managed instances at scale through an IAM-controlled API call, distinct from Session Manager's per-instance interactive shell. A patch baseline defines which patches apply during patching, and Parameter Store holds configuration values - neither executes arbitrary commands at scale.$q$),
('sc-q-421','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A compliance requirement mandates that every managed EC2 instance report its current OS patch compliance status, and that any missing critical patches be automatically installed during a defined maintenance window. Which Systems Manager capability satisfies this?$q$,$q$["Patch Manager","Session Manager","Parameter Store","Distributor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Patch Manager automates scanning instances against a patch baseline, reports compliance status, and installs missing patches during a scheduled maintenance window - directly matching the requirement. Session Manager provides interactive access, Parameter Store stores configuration values, and Distributor packages and distributes software/agent packages rather than managing OS patch compliance.$q$),
('sc-q-422','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A security team wants any managed instance whose configuration drifts from a required baseline (for example, a required security agent gets stopped) to be automatically detected and returned to the desired state without a human intervening. Which Systems Manager capability fits?$q$,$q$["State Manager associations","Session Manager","A one-time Run Command execution","AWS CloudTrail"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$State Manager associations continuously enforce a defined configuration state on managed instances and automatically remediate drift back to that state on a recurring schedule. Session Manager provides interactive access rather than ongoing enforcement, a one-time Run Command execution only applies once and won't catch later drift, and CloudTrail only logs API activity - it doesn't enforce configuration.$q$),
('sc-q-423','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A team wants developers to be able to start ordinary Session Manager shell sessions on instances tagged Environment=dev, but must prevent those same developers from using Session Manager's port-forwarding session type against any instance. How should this be enforced?$q$,$q$["An IAM policy using ssm:StartSession conditions that scope access to the standard shell session document and the dev-tagged instances, while explicitly denying the port-forwarding session document","There is no way to distinguish session types in IAM policy","A security group rule that blocks port forwarding","Disabling Session Manager for the account entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$IAM policies can condition ssm:StartSession on the specific SSM document used to start the session (standard shell vs. port-forwarding documents) combined with resource tag conditions, allowing fine-grained control over both which instances and which session type a principal may use. Security groups operate at the network layer and have no visibility into Session Manager's session type, and disabling Session Manager entirely would remove the legitimate shell access the team still needs.$q$),
('sc-q-424','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$What does AWS Systems Manager Session Manager's port forwarding feature provide?$q$,$q$["A tunnel from a local port to a specific port on the remote managed instance (such as RDP or a database port), without opening any inbound security group rule","A way to forward the instance's public IP address to a load balancer","An automatic new inbound Network ACL rule","A method to copy files using SCP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Port forwarding tunnels a local port to a port on the remote instance through the existing Systems Manager channel, giving access to services like RDP or a database port without opening any inbound rule on the instance's security group. It doesn't touch NACLs, doesn't forward public IPs to a load balancer, and isn't a file-copy mechanism.$q$),
('sc-q-425','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$Why does IMDSv1 pose a risk when an application running on the instance has a server-side request forgery (SSRF) vulnerability?$q$,$q$["IMDSv1 answers any simple GET request sent to the metadata address with instance metadata, including the attached role's temporary credentials, with no session or token required","IMDSv1 does not expose any credentials under any circumstances","The instance metadata address is only reachable from outside the VPC","SSRF vulnerabilities cannot reach the instance metadata service under IMDSv1"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$IMDSv1 responds to a plain GET request with instance metadata, including the IAM role's temporary credentials, requiring no prior token or session. An SSRF flaw that lets an attacker control a server-side GET request can therefore be pointed at the metadata address to steal those credentials with a single request - exactly the risk IMDSv2's token requirement is designed to close.$q$),
('sc-q-426','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$When IMDSv2 is enforced (required) on an instance, what must a client do to successfully retrieve data from the Instance Metadata Service?$q$,$q$["Send only a GET request, exactly as with IMDSv1","First send a PUT request to obtain a session token, then include that token in a header on subsequent GET requests","Present the instance's long-term IAM access key","Nothing changes; IMDSv2 behaves identically to IMDSv1"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$IMDSv2 requires a PUT request to obtain a short-lived session token before any metadata GET request will succeed, and that token must be included as a header on the follow-up GET. A plain GET alone (the IMDSv1 pattern) is rejected once IMDSv2 is required, and the instance has no long-term IAM access key to present in the first place - it authenticates via the temporary role credentials the metadata service itself issues.$q$),
('sc-q-427','awsscs',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Setting the instance metadata service's http-put-response-hop-limit to 1 primarily defends against which scenario?$q$,$q$["A containerized application on the instance forwarding the IMDSv2 token request through an internal proxy or extra network hop to reach credentials it shouldn't have access to","Slow network performance between the instance and the metadata service","The instance running out of available private IP addresses","Excessive CloudTrail log volume from metadata requests"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The hop limit caps how many network hops (such as through a container's network namespace or an internal proxy) the metadata request's IP packet can traverse before being dropped; setting it to 1 prevents a process running one hop away - such as inside a container - from successfully forwarding a token request to reach credentials meant only for the host. It has no relationship to network speed, IP address exhaustion, or CloudTrail volume.$q$),
('sc-q-428','awsscs',3,'3.3','multi',3,$q$Which of the following are genuine security benefits of using SSM Session Manager instead of a traditional SSH bastion host for administrative access to EC2 instances? (Select TWO)$q$,$q$["No inbound security group rule is required on the managed instance for administrative access","Access can be governed entirely through IAM policy instead of distributing and managing SSH key pairs","It eliminates the need for the administrator to hold any IAM permissions at all","It guarantees that the instance's operating system is fully patched"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Session Manager needs no inbound port because the instance initiates an outbound connection to Systems Manager, and access is controlled entirely by IAM policy rather than distributed SSH keys - both genuine, structural security improvements over a bastion. It does not eliminate the need for IAM permissions (the administrator still needs an IAM identity permitted to call ssm:StartSession), and it says nothing about patch level - that's a separate concern handled by tools like Patch Manager.$q$),
('sc-q-429','awsscs',3,'3.3','ordering',2,$q$A compliance review needs to reconstruct exactly what happened during a specific past Session Manager session. Order the steps to build that audit trail.$q$,$q$["Locate the session ID and the initiating IAM principal from the CloudTrail StartSession event","Confirm the SSM document used for that session had CloudWatch Logs and/or S3 logging enabled","Retrieve the streamed session log content (commands and output) from the configured logging destination","Correlate the retrieved log content with the CloudTrail timestamps to reconstruct the full session timeline"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$You first find which session and which principal are in scope from the CloudTrail StartSession event, then confirm the session's document had logging enabled (without which no content exists to retrieve), then pull the actual streamed commands/output from CloudWatch Logs or S3, and finally line that content up against the CloudTrail timestamps to produce a complete, ordered account of the session.$q$),
('sc-q-430','awsscs',3,'3.3','matching',2,$q$Match each AWS Systems Manager capability to what it is primarily used for.$q$,$q${"left":["Session Manager","Run Command","Patch Manager","State Manager"],"right":["Interactive, fully audited shell access to an instance with no inbound ports required","Executing one-off or scheduled commands and scripts across many instances via an API call","Scanning for and installing OS/application patches across a fleet on a schedule","Continuously enforcing and self-healing a defined configuration state on managed instances"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Session Manager provides interactive, logged shell access without inbound ports; Run Command executes commands/scripts at scale via API; Patch Manager handles fleet-wide patch scanning and installation; and State Manager continuously enforces and auto-remediates a defined configuration baseline - four complementary but distinct Systems Manager capabilities.$q$);
